  "text": "Let $P(x) = ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d$ be the polynomial in question.\n\nFrom $P(0) = k$, we get $d = k$.\n\nFrom $P(1) = 2k$, we get $a + b + c + d = 2k \\Rightarrow a + b + c + k = 2k \\Rightarrow a + b + c = k$.\n\nFrom $P(-1) = 3k$, we get $-a + b - c + d = 3k \\Rightarrow -a + b - c + k = 3k \\Rightarrow -a + b - c = 2k$.\n\nNow we have a system of equations:\n\n\\begin{align*}\na + b + c &= k, \\\\\n-a + b - c &= 2k, \\\\\na^3 - 3ab^2 - 3ac^2 + 3abc + b^3 - 3b^2c + 3b^2k + k &= 0.\n\\end{align*}\n\nAdding the two equations from $a + b + c = k$ and $-a + b - c = 2k$ gives us $2b = 3k$.\n\nFrom the third equation, we get:\n\n$a^3 - 3ab(a+b) - 3a^2ck + b^3(a+b) + 3abc = -k(a+b)$.\n\nSubstituting $2b = 3k$ into this equation, we get:\n\n$a^3 - 3a(3k - b)a - 3a^2ck + b^3(3k - b) + 3abc = -k(a+b)$.\n\nSimplifying further:\n\n$a^3 - 9a^2k + 3ab^2 + 3b^3k - 3b^2k - 3a^2ck = -k(a+b) + 3abc$.\n\nSince $2b = 3k$, we get $b = \\frac{3k}{2}$. Substituting this value of $b$ into the polynomial:\n\n$k = 0$,\n\nwe get:\n\n$a^3 - 9a(3k - \\frac{3k}{2}) - 3a^2(\\frac{3k}{2}) + (\\frac{3k}{2})^3 - 3(\\frac{3k}{2})^2k - 3a(\\frac{3k}{2}c) = 0$.\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get:\n\n$- \\frac{45a^2}{4} k - \\frac{9a^2}{4} k - \\frac{27a}{4} k c + \\frac{27k^3}{8} - \\frac{27k^3}{4} - \\frac{9a}{2} k c = 0$.\n\nSince $k \\neq 0$, we must have:\n\n$- \\frac{63a^2}{4} k - \\frac{27a}{4} k c + \\frac{27k^3}{8} - \\frac{9a}{2} k c = 0$.\n\nTaking the derivative of $P(x)$ with respect to $x$ gives us $P'(x) = 3ax^2 + 2bx + c$. Since we want three distinct real solutions, we must have one or both of the following points in $P'(x)$: $a = 0$ and $b = 0$. Without loss of generality, let's assume $a = 0$ and $b = 0$. This gives us:\n\n$- \\frac{27c}{4} k + \\frac{27k^3}{8} - \\frac{9k c}{2} = 0$.\n\nSince $k \\neq 0$, we get:\n\n$- \\frac{27c}{4} + \\frac{27k^2}{8} - \\frac{9c}{2} = 0$.\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get:\n\n$c = \\frac{9k^2}{8} - \\frac{27k}{8}$.\n\nSince we want $k$ to be as large as possible, we must have $c > 0$. This gives us:\n\n$k^2 - 3k - 6 < 0$.\n\nTaking the square root of both sides and rearranging, we get:\n\n$k^2 - 3k < 6$.\n\nSince $k < k^2$, we must have $k^2 - 3k < 6$. This inequality holds for $k < \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{33}}{2}$. Therefore, the largest integer value of $k$ that satisfies this inequality is $\\boxed{k = 2}$.",
